notes               = "Untitled in source; the dedication به فیروز شیروانلو is printed in the upper left of the page and is not part of the poem body. The roles reverse halfway: the painter is the one asking to be painted. Shirvanlou (1938–1988) was the translator and cultural organiser behind much of the Kanoon's work for children; if those dates are right, this autumn-1368 poem addresses a man a year dead, which would account for a void with no air in it to carry a voice. Offered as a reading, not a fact. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

گفتم
تو را دوست دارم
صدای مرا نقاشی کن
دلتنگ توام
اندوه مرا نقاشی کن
به تو می‌اندیشم در غمِ دیگران
پندارِ مرا نقاشی کن.
گفتی
در خلائی که هوا نیست
نه من تو را می‌خوانم
نه تو مرا می‌شنوی
برایم چراغی بیاور
بی‌نور چگونه نقاشی کنم.
پاییز ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

I said
I love you
paint my voice
I am lonely for you
paint my sorrow
I think of you in the grief of others
paint my imagining.
You said
in a void where there is no air
neither do I call you
nor do you hear me
bring me a lamp
without light, how am I to paint.
Autumn 1368

===lantern===

I said:
I love you,
paint my voice.
I am heartsick for you,
paint my sorrow.
I think of you inside other people's grief,
paint what I imagine.
You said:
in a void where there is no air
I do not call you
and you do not hear me.
Bring me a lamp —
how am I to paint without light?

Autumn 1368

